Subject: [PATCH] powerpc/powernv/pci: enable the TVE#0 64-bit DMA bypass on PHB4

A PE's TVE#0 covers only the first 4GB of PCI address space.  Reaching
past that needs bit 59 of the PCI address set, which selects TVE#1, so a
device whose DMA mask cannot reach bit 59 - which is to say almost every
commodity PCIe device, 44 to 48 bits being typical - is confined to the
PE's 32-bit TCE window.  On a POWER9 that window is 2GB:

  pci 0030:03 : [PE# 1fb] Setting up 32-bit TCE table at 0..80000000

pnv_pci_ioda_dma_64bit_bypass() exists to fix exactly this, remapping
TVE#0 into a large identity window above 4GB, but it is gated on
phb->model == PNV_PHB_MODEL_PHB3 and a POWER9 never matches: the model is
only ever set from "ibm,p7ioc-pciex" or "ibm,power8-pciex", so a PHB4
("ibm,power9-pciex", "ibm,ioda3-phb") ends up PNV_PHB_MODEL_UNKNOWN.

The result is not a slow path, it is a hard failure under DMA pressure.
A driver that sizes its DMA aperture from system memory rather than from
the IOMMU window will accept far more mapping than 2GB can translate -
amdgpu announces "228789M of GTT memory ready" on a 447GiB host, a 112x
overcommit - and the PHB fences the PE on the resulting bad IOVA:

  EEH: Frozen PE#0 on PHB#3 detected
  PHB4 PHB#3 Diag-data
  RxeTceErr:  6000000000000000 2000000000000000 c000000000000000

Add PNV_PHB_MODEL_PHB4 and allow the remap for it.

The model is a new enum value rather than a reuse of PNV_PHB_MODEL_PHB3
deliberately: three other sites test for PHB3 to reach direct-MMIO TCE
invalidation (pnv_pci_phb3_tce_invalidate{,_pe}) and an MSI EOI path that
do not apply to PHB4, and those must keep taking their OPAL fallbacks.
Relabelling POWER9 as PHB3 would drive PHB3's register layout against
PHB4 hardware.

Also take the TCE size from the PHB rather than hardcoding 256M.  256M is
a POWER8-only default in pnv_ioda_parse_tce_sizes(); a PHB4 advertises
4K/64K/2M/1G in "ibm,supported-tce-sizes", and OPAL rejects a window built
with a size the PHB does not support, so the hardcoded order would have
made this fail on PHB4 even once the model gate passed.  Taking the
largest advertised size that fits the window also keeps the table small:
1G TCEs over a 1TiB window is 1024 entries, 8KB.

On PHB3 the selection is a no-op: where "ibm,supported-tce-sizes" is
absent pnv_ioda_parse_tce_sizes() returns 4K|64K|16M|256M, whose top bit
is 256M, so POWER8 gets exactly the size it got from the old hardcode.

This does not relax the "(pe->device_count == 1 || !pe->pbus)" condition.
The remap leaves the low 4GB of the PE's PCI address space unmapped, so it
stays restricted to PEs where no other device may want 32-bit DMA.

--- a/arch/powerpc/platforms/powernv/pci.h
+++ b/arch/powerpc/platforms/powernv/pci.h
@@ -19,6 +19,7 @@
 	PNV_PHB_MODEL_UNKNOWN,
 	PNV_PHB_MODEL_P7IOC,
 	PNV_PHB_MODEL_PHB3,
+	PNV_PHB_MODEL_PHB4,
 };
 
 #define PNV_PCI_DIAG_BUF_SIZE	8192
--- a/arch/powerpc/platforms/powernv/pci-ioda.c
+++ b/arch/powerpc/platforms/powernv/pci-ioda.c
@@ -1048,13 +1048,14 @@
  * by 64-bit DMAs.  This should only be used by devices that want more than
  * 4GB, and only on PEs that have no 32-bit devices.
  *
- * Currently this will only work on PHB3 (POWER8).
+ * This works on PHB3 (POWER8) and PHB4 (POWER9).
  */
 static int pnv_pci_ioda_dma_64bit_bypass(struct pnv_ioda_pe *pe)
 {
 	u64 window_size, table_size, tce_count, addr;
 	struct page *table_pages;
-	u64 tce_order = 28; /* 256MB TCEs */
+	unsigned long pgsizes;
+	u64 tce_order;
 	__be64 *tces;
 	s64 rc;
 
@@ -1063,6 +1064,19 @@
 	 * shifting memory by the 4GB offset required to skip 32bit space.
 	 */
 	window_size = roundup_pow_of_two(memory_hotplug_max() + (1ULL << 32));
+
+	/*
+	 * Use the largest TCE the PHB advertises that still fits the window,
+	 * so the table stays small.  This was hardcoded to 256M, which only
+	 * works because pnv_ioda_parse_tce_sizes() defaults 256M in on
+	 * POWER8; a PHB4 advertises 4K/64K/2M/1G in ibm,supported-tce-sizes
+	 * and OPAL rejects a window built with a size it does not support.
+	 */
+	pgsizes = pe->table_group.pgsizes & ((window_size << 1) - 1);
+	if (!pgsizes)
+		return -EIO;
+	tce_order = ilog2(pgsizes);
+
 	tce_count = window_size >> tce_order;
 	table_size = tce_count << 3;
 
@@ -1080,7 +1094,7 @@
 
 	memset(tces, 0, table_size);
 
-	for (addr = 0; addr < memory_hotplug_max(); addr += (1 << tce_order)) {
+	for (addr = 0; addr < memory_hotplug_max(); addr += (1ULL << tce_order)) {
 		tces[(addr + (1ULL << 32)) >> tce_order] =
 			cpu_to_be64(addr | TCE_PCI_READ | TCE_PCI_WRITE);
 	}
@@ -1129,7 +1143,8 @@
 	    dma_mask > (memory_hotplug_max() + (1ULL << 32)) &&
 	    /* pe->pdev should be set if it's a single device, pe->pbus if not */
 	    (pe->device_count == 1 || !pe->pbus) &&
-	    phb->model == PNV_PHB_MODEL_PHB3) {
+	    (phb->model == PNV_PHB_MODEL_PHB3 ||
+	     phb->model == PNV_PHB_MODEL_PHB4)) {
 		/* Configure the bypass mode */
 		s64 rc = pnv_pci_ioda_dma_64bit_bypass(pe);
 		if (rc)
@@ -2544,6 +2559,8 @@
 		phb->model = PNV_PHB_MODEL_P7IOC;
 	else if (of_device_is_compatible(np, "ibm,power8-pciex"))
 		phb->model = PNV_PHB_MODEL_PHB3;
+	else if (of_device_is_compatible(np, "ibm,power9-pciex"))
+		phb->model = PNV_PHB_MODEL_PHB4;
 	else
 		phb->model = PNV_PHB_MODEL_UNKNOWN;
 
